 "literal": "In the dotted shade of the fork of the inner path / O warrior who dries the whole thick flesh! / You are indeed sulking / Indeed, many are those who sat waiting for your sign!",
 "translation": "O warrior who dries your entire thick flesh in the dappled shade of the fork of the inner path! You are indeed sulking. But those who sat waiting for your sign to die with you are indeed many!",
 "modern": "உள்ளே செல்லும் வழியின் கவர்த்த வழியில் உள்ள புள்ளிகளையுடைய நிழலில், உன்னுடைய முழுமையான தசைப்பற்றை உண்ணாநோன்பிருந்து காயவைக்கும் வீரனே! நீ பிணங்கி இருக்கிறாய். ஆனால், உன்னுடைய குறிப்பறிந்து உன்னோடு வடக்கிருக்க அமர்ந்தவர்கள் பலர் ஆவர்!",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai and Kaiyaṟunilai tuṟai, composed by Peruṅkaruvūrccatukkattu Pūtanātaṉār, addressed to Kōpperuñcōḻaṉ. The king, deeply hurt by the rebellion of his sons, decided to end his life by the ritual of fasting facing north (vaṭakkiruttal). The poet addresses the king as a warrior who is drying his flesh through fasting in the dappled shade of a path's fork. He notes that the king is sulking or aggrieved by his sons' behavior, which led him to this extreme step. However, the poet comforts him by pointing out that he is not alone, as many loyal friends and poets have also sat down to fast and die along with him, showing their deep love and solidarity.",
